# ============================================================================
#  .htaccess.example — Apache equivalent of deploy/nginx.conf.example
# ============================================================================
#
#  Install (SAME subfolder install at /portfolio, document root /var/www/html):
#    sudo cp deploy/.htaccess.example /var/www/html/portfolio/.htaccess
#    sudo chown www-data:www-data /var/www/html/portfolio/.htaccess
#    sudo chmod 644 /var/www/html/portfolio/.htaccess
#
#    READ THE AllowOverride NOTE BELOW FIRST, or this file will do nothing
#    at all and you will spend an afternoon wondering why.
#
#  READ THIS BEFORE THE FIRST DEPLOY: deploy.sh runs
#    rsync -a --delete dist/ /var/www/html/portfolio/
#  and `--delete` removes every file in the target that is not in ./dist. A
#  .htaccess copied into the deploy directory BY HAND is therefore gone again
#  after the next ./deploy.sh. Two ways to live with that:
#    (a) re-run the `cp` above after every deploy, or
#    (b) keep the file in the build: copy it to public/.htaccess once
#        (`cp deploy/.htaccess.example public/.htaccess`) and rebuild. Vite
#        copies everything in public/ into dist/ — dotfiles included — so
#        `npm run build` then puts the .htaccess into dist/ and `rsync --delete`
#        keeps it. Option (b) is the one that survives on its own.
#
#  Assumptions:
#    - deploy.sh copies dist/ into /var/www/html/portfolio
#    - the site is reached at https://your-subdomain.example/portfolio/
#    - TLS is terminated by certbot (see the README, section 6)
#
#  If you install somewhere other than /portfolio, change two places:
#  RewriteBase /portfolio/ and the RewriteCond/Redirect target below.
# ============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AllowOverride — THE prerequisite, and the most common silent failure
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .htaccess is only read when the enclosing vhost says so:
#
#   <Directory /var/www/html>
#       AllowOverride All
#   </Directory>
#
# (or equivalently /var/www/html/portfolio, if you want it scoped tighter)
#
# THE TRAP: with `AllowOverride None` — the Apache default on Debian/Ubuntu
# since 2.4 — Apache does NOT warn and does NOT error. It simply never reads
# the file. The site still works, gzip still works if it was configured in the
# vhost, and every line below is dead. If you change one thing here, change
# this.
#
# You do not need `All` forever; the narrow setting this file actually needs is
# `AllowOverride Options=Indexes,MultiViews,FollowSymLinks FileInfo=...` plus
# RewriteEngine, which is painful to remember — that is why `All` is used here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Modules that must be enabled
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   sudo a2enmod rewrite headers expires deflate
#   sudo systemctl reload apache2
#
# Brotli needs a third-party module and is at the bottom of this file.
#
# "Options not allowed here" or "FileInfo not allowed here" means AllowOverride
# again (see above), not a typo. RewriteEngine in .htaccess additionally needs
# AllowOverride FileInfo or All.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Directory behaviour
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Never let a visitor list directory contents: without -Indexes, a directory
# URL that happens to resolve (e.g. /portfolio/assets/) is a full file listing.
Options -Indexes

# Serve index.html for the directory. Together with mod_dir this also turns a
# request for /portfolio into /portfolio/index.html.
DirectoryIndex index.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPA fallback — SCOPED TO THE SUBFOLDER, on purpose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same safety concern as the nginx `location /portfolio/` block: a rewrite that
# matches EVERY path must never live above this directory, or it swallows the
# other sites in /var/www/html. A single-page app needs a fallback to its own
# index.html for deep links, and it needs that and nothing else.
RewriteEngine On

# The URL prefix this directory is served at. Used by the redirect below.
RewriteBase /portfolio/

# GUARD — belt and braces, and the Apache-side version of the nginx scoping.
# If this file is ever pasted into /var/www/html/.htaccess by mistake, this
# condition makes every request that is NOT under /portfolio/ skip all the rules
# below untouched, instead of being rewritten to this portfolio.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/portfolio/ [NC]
RewriteRule ^ - [L]

# Redirect /portfolio (no trailing slash) to /portfolio/. mod_dir's
# DirectorySlash normally does this already; the rule is here so the fix does
# not depend on that. Note the trailing slash in the target — `/portfolio`
# without it is the classic subfolder-install bug that shows up as a 404.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
RewriteRule ^$ /portfolio/ [R=301,L]

# The fallback itself: if the requested path is NOT a real file and NOT a real
# directory, hand back the app shell and let React render the route.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
RewriteRule ^ index.html [L]

# NOTE ON SCOPE: because this file lives in /var/www/html/portfolio/, mod_rewrite
# strips that prefix before matching, so `^ index.html` can only ever match
# paths under /portfolio/. In the DOCUMENT ROOT's .htaccess the same line would
# match every site on the server — which is exactly why RewriteBase and the
# !^/portfolio/ guard above exist.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mod_deflate — the same mime types as the nginx gzip block
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AddOutputFilterByType needs AllowOverride FileInfo (or All).
# Already-compressed types (image/png, image/jpeg, application/zip) are
# deliberately absent: deflating them costs CPU and saves nothing.
<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/plain
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/css
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/xml
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/javascript
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/javascript
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/json
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/xml
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/rss+xml
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE image/svg+xml
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE font/woff
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE font/woff2
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mod_expires — the cache policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two halves that matter:
#   - hashed build assets (assets/index-<hash>.js) get ONE YEAR, because the
#     hash is part of the filename, so the bytes behind that name never change;
#   - index.html is NEVER cached, so a new deploy actually reaches visitors
#     instead of hiding behind a stale HTML that points at old asset hashes.
<IfModule mod_expires.c>
    ExpiresActive On

    # The default for anything not matched below. One month is deliberately
    # modest; it also applies to index.html, which the Cache-Control block in
    # the next section then overrides. Do NOT set
    # `ExpiresDefault "access plus 1 year"` here as a shortcut — that is how
    # index.html ends up cached for a year and nobody can receive your deploy.
    ExpiresDefault "access plus 1 month"

    # --- hashed build assets: one year ------------------------------------
    # Vite writes files like assets/index-C_bwcQqY.js. The 8-character group
    # after the last dash is the content hash. The precision matters: a blanket
    # `*.js` / `*.svg` rule would also give the unhashed favicon.svg an
    # immutable year, and then a new favicon could never reach a visitor who
    # has already been there.
    ExpiresByType text/javascript          "access plus 1 year"
    ExpiresByType application/javascript   "access plus 1 year"
    ExpiresByType text/css                  "access plus 1 year"
    ExpiresByType image/svg+xml             "access plus 1 year"
    ExpiresByType font/woff                 "access plus 1 year"
    ExpiresByType font/woff2                "access plus 1 year"
    ExpiresByType application/font-woff     "access plus 1 year"
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cache-Control refinements — these need mod_headers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mod_expires cannot emit the `immutable` token; mod_headers can. mod_headers
# runs at fixup time, i.e. AFTER mod_expires, so `Header set` replaces the
# Cache-Control line mod_expires just generated and leaves its `Expires` date in
# place — which is what you should see in `curl -I`.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    # Hashed assets: one year, immutable.
    <FilesMatch "-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8}\.(js|css)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
    </FilesMatch>

    # The social preview image changes only when it is regenerated, so a week
    # is plenty. FilesMatch matches the BASENAME, hence the ^ and $.
    <FilesMatch "^og-image\.png$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=604800"
    </FilesMatch>

    # index.html is the app shell: never cached, only revalidated.
    <FilesMatch "^index\.html$">
        Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
        Header set Pragma "no-cache"
    </FilesMatch>

    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Security headers
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Same trap as nginx `add_header`, and it is worth reading twice.
    #
    # nginx:   an `add_header` inside a `location` block DROPS every header
    #          inherited from the server block.
    # Apache:  a `Header` directive inside <FilesMatch> / <Location> /
    #          <Directory> REPLACES the inherited set for the requests it
    #          matches. The directives are not merged with the outer ones — the
    #          outer ones simply stop applying to those requests.
    #
    # Consequence for this file: the <FilesMatch> blocks above set Cache-Control
    # for assets and for index.html. If those responses must ALSO carry the
    # security headers below, the headers have to be repeated inside each block
    # (the same "repeat them in every block" fix the nginx example uses), or
    # the security headers must be hoisted into the vhost — or into a separate
    # file that every block includes. Both are fine; silently assuming they
    # merge is not.
    #
    # `always` on Apache is not nginx's "also on error responses" keyword: here
    # it means "apply this header to every response, including ones generated
    # by other modules" (a 404 from mod_rewrite, for instance). Its opposite
    # is `Header onsuccess set`, which only covers successful responses.
    Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    Header always set X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
    Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
    Header always set Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()"

    # Content Security Policy.
    # 'unsafe-inline' in style-src is REQUIRED by this project: Tailwind injects
    # inline <style> blocks at runtime and Framer Motion sets element .style
    # properties directly, so a strict style-src breaks the styling.
    # TO TIGHTEN IT LATER: drop 'unsafe-inline' and serve a per-request nonce
    # instead (mod_csp, or Header set with a random value generated per
    # request), and move the 12-line theme bootstrap in index.html into a real
    # file. Only worth it once the runtime style injection is gone.
    # script-src does NOT need 'unsafe-inline': the only inline <script> is that
    # theme bootstrap, which has no inline event handlers.
    Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'"

    # HSTS: only enable once HTTPS really works, otherwise you can lock
    # yourself out of the site for months.
    # Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Brotli — OPTIONAL, and only if mod_brotli is installed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   sudo apt-get install -y libapache2-mod-brotli
#   sudo a2enmod brotli
#   sudo systemctl reload apache2
#
# Brotli compresses better than gzip for JS/CSS, which is most of this site.
# The <IfModule mod_brotli.c> guard is what makes the block safe: with the
# module missing, Apache skips the block instead of refusing to start. Without
# the guard an unknown output filter is a fatal "AH00526: Syntax error" and the
# server does not come back up — so do not uncomment the filter without the
# module.
#
# <IfModule mod_brotli.c>
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS text/html
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS text/plain
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS text/css
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS text/javascript
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS application/javascript
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS application/json
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS image/svg+xml
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS font/woff
#     AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS font/woff2
#     AddType ".br" .br
# </IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Checking that it worked
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   sudo apachectl configtest
#   sudo systemctl reload apache2
#   curl -I http://127.0.0.1/portfolio/                  # 200 + the headers above
#   curl -I http://127.0.0.1/portfolio/assets/index-*.js # immutable, 1 year
#
# If the security headers are missing while Expires and gzip work, read the
# AllowOverride note at the top again — then remember that a `Header` inside
# <FilesMatch> REPLACES the outer headers for matching files instead of adding
# to them, so the security headers have to be repeated inside that block.